+    assigned the constant once and only ever borrowed immutably. The borrows may
+    thus outlive the function (e.g. [fn f(..) -> &'a [T] { &[] }]): the loans of
+    such locals are not ended when the frame is popped, but kept in anonymous
+    values, like the loans of string literals.
+
+    A local qualifies if it is neither the return value nor an input, it is
+    assigned exactly once, as a whole, with a constant or an aggregate of
+    constants, and every other use of it is a shared borrow (or a storage
+    marker). As no loan of it can be ended by a write, keeping its loans alive
+    after the frame is popped only makes them outlive the function, which borrow
+    checking guarantees is only observable for promoted constants. *)
